{
  "courseSlug": "introduction-to-commodity-trading-gold-silver-crude-and-agri-commodities",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "title": "Chapter 2: How Commodity Derivatives Work",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "title": "Spot, Futures and Basis: Understanding Contango and Backwardation",
          "slug": "spot-futures-and-basis-understanding-contango-and-backwardation",
          "body": [
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-001",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-001s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Open the MCX gold contracts on any given day and you will usually see each later month priced a little higher than the one before it. Many beginners read that as the market predicting gold will rise. It is not. Open crude oil during a supply crunch and you may see the opposite, with later months cheaper, and that is not a forecast of falling oil either. By the end of this lesson you will know what really sets the gap between spot and futures prices, what contango and backwardation mean, why every futures price converges to spot at expiry, and how the shape of the curve quietly adds to or subtracts from your returns.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-002",
              "_type": "bigIdea",
              "text": "A futures price is not a forecast. It is mostly today's spot price plus the cost of holding the commodity until expiry, adjusted for how badly the market needs the physical goods right now."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-003",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-003s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Spot, Futures and Basis",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-004",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-004m1",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "The price for buying or selling a commodity for immediate delivery, in the physical market today."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-004m2",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "The difference between the spot price and the futures price of a commodity. In this course, basis = spot minus futures."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-004s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-004s2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "spot price",
                  "marks": [
                    "c2l3-004m1"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-004s3",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " is what the commodity costs for immediate delivery today. The futures price is the price agreed today for delivery on a later date. The gap between them is the ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-004s4",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "basis",
                  "marks": [
                    "c2l3-004m2"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-004s5",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-005",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "\\text{Basis} = \\text{Spot price} - \\text{Futures price}",
              "caption": "Conventions differ, and some traders define basis as futures minus spot. This course uses spot minus futures throughout."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-006",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-006s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "If spot gold is ₹1,00,000 per 10 grams and the December futures trade at ₹1,01,150, the basis is ₹1,00,000 minus ₹1,01,150 = minus ₹1,150. A negative basis means futures are above spot.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-007",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-007s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Cost of Carry: Where the Gap Comes From",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-008",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-008m1",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "The total cost of holding a physical commodity until a future date, mainly interest on the money tied up, plus storage and insurance."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-008s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Imagine you need 1 kg of gold in two months. You have two choices: buy futures now, or buy physical gold today and store it. If both routes lead to the same gold on the same date, they should cost about the same. Buying today means paying now, so you lose interest on that money, and you pay for storage and insurance. That total is the ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-008s2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "cost of carry",
                  "marks": [
                    "c2l3-008m1"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-008s3",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ", and futures prices build it in.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-009",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "F \\approx S \\times \\left(1 + r \\times \\frac{d}{365}\\right) + \\text{Storage and insurance} - \\text{Convenience yield}",
              "caption": "F = futures price, S = spot price, r = annual interest rate, d = days to expiry. Convenience yield is explained below."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-010",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h3",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-010s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Worked Example: Gold",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-011",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-011s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Spot gold is ₹1,00,000 per 10 grams, interest is 7% a year, and the contract expires in 60 days. Gold is compact and cheap to store, so we ignore storage here.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-012",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-012s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Interest cost: ₹1,00,000 × 0.07 × 60 ÷ 365 = ₹1,151 (rounded)",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-013",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-013s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Fair futures price: ₹1,00,000 + ₹1,151 = ₹1,01,151",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-014",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-014s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "If the futures traded far above that, arbitrageurs would buy spot and sell futures. Far below, they would do the reverse. That is the cash and carry trade you met in Chapter 1, and it keeps gold futures close to fair value.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-015",
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "numericInput",
              "title": "Price a silver future",
              "prompt": "Spot silver is ₹1,20,000 per kg. Interest is 6.5% a year, the contract expires in 90 days, and storage plus insurance costs ₹150 per kg for the period. Ignoring convenience yield, what is the fair futures price per kg, to the nearest rupee?",
              "correctValue": 122073,
              "tolerance": 5,
              "unit": "₹",
              "explanation": "Interest cost = ₹1,20,000 × 0.065 × 90 ÷ 365 = ₹1,923. Add storage of ₹150. Fair futures price = ₹1,20,000 + ₹1,923 + ₹150 = ₹1,22,073 per kg."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-016",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-016s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Contango: The Normal Upward Slope",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-017",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-017m1",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "A market structure in which futures prices are higher than the spot price, and later-expiry contracts are priced higher than nearer ones, usually reflecting the cost of carry."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-017s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "When futures trade above spot, and later months trade above earlier ones, the market is in ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-017s2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "contango",
                  "marks": [
                    "c2l3-017m1"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-017s3",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ". For storable commodities with plentiful supply, contango is the normal state, because carrying costs are real and positive.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-018",
              "_type": "chart",
              "chartType": "line",
              "title": "Illustrative gold futures curve in contango (₹ per 10 grams, 7% carry)",
              "xAxisLabel": "Contract",
              "yAxisLabel": "₹ per 10 grams",
              "data": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-018d1",
                  "label": "Spot",
                  "value": 100000
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-018d2",
                  "label": "30 days",
                  "value": 100575
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-018d3",
                  "label": "90 days",
                  "value": 101726
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-018d4",
                  "label": "150 days",
                  "value": 102877
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-018d5",
                  "label": "210 days",
                  "value": 104027
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-019",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-019s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Each point is simply spot plus seven percent a year of interest for the days to expiry. Nothing in this curve says gold will be ₹1,04,027 in seven months. It says that is what it costs to lock in gold for that date today.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-020",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-020s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Backwardation: When the Market Wants It Now",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-021",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-021m1",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "A market structure in which futures prices are lower than the spot price, and later-expiry contracts are cheaper than nearer ones, usually because physical supply is tight right now."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-021m2",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "The benefit of physically holding a commodity now, such as keeping a refinery or mill running during a shortage. When it is high, it can push futures below spot."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-021s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Sometimes futures trade below spot, and later months are cheaper than nearer ones. This is ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-021s2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "backwardation",
                  "marks": [
                    "c2l3-021m1"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-021s3",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ". It seems to break the cost of carry logic, until you add the missing piece: ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-021s4",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "convenience yield",
                  "marks": [
                    "c2l3-021m2"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-021s5",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-022",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-022s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "When supply is tight, holding the physical commodity today is valuable. A refinery that runs out of crude has to shut down. A spinning mill without cotton stops production. Those businesses will pay a premium for barrels and bales now, bidding up spot and near-month prices above later months.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-023",
              "_type": "chart",
              "chartType": "line",
              "title": "Illustrative crude oil futures curve in backwardation (₹ per barrel)",
              "xAxisLabel": "Contract",
              "yAxisLabel": "₹ per barrel",
              "data": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-023d1",
                  "label": "Spot",
                  "value": 7000
                },
                {
