            {
              "_key": "c2l3-030",
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "fillInTheBlank",
              "title": "What happens at expiry?",
              "textWithBlank": "As a futures contract approaches expiry, its price ___ towards the spot price, and the basis shrinks to zero.",
              "correctAnswers": [
                "converges",
                "moves",
                "converge"
              ],
              "explanation": "At expiry, futures and spot describe the same thing: the commodity for immediate delivery. Any gap left would be free money for arbitrageurs, so it disappears."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-031",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-031s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Roll Yield: The Hidden Return From Curve Shape",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-032",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-032m1",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "The gain or loss from futures prices converging towards spot over time, felt by traders who hold and roll positions. Negative in contango for longs, positive in backwardation for longs."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-032s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Convergence has a direct effect on anyone who holds futures for long periods by rolling. This effect is called ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-032s2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "roll yield",
                  "marks": [
                    "c2l3-032m1"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-032s3",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-033",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-033s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "In contango, longs lose. ",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-033s2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "You keep buying the next, more expensive contract, and it keeps drifting down towards spot. If spot is flat, your futures position loses money every cycle.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-034",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-034s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "In backwardation, longs gain. ",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-034s2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "You keep buying the next, cheaper contract, and it drifts up towards spot. Even with flat spot prices, your position makes money.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-035",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-035s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "For shorts, it is the reverse. ",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-035s2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "A short position benefits from contango and suffers in backwardation.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-041",
              "_type": "collapsible",
              "title": "Show me the math: a year of rolling in contango",
              "content": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-036",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-036s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Say spot gold stays exactly at ₹1,00,000 for a year, and each two-month contract is priced at ₹1,01,151 using 7% carry. A long trader buys each contract at ₹1,01,151 and holds it to expiry, when it converges to ₹1,00,000.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-037",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1,
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-037s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Loss per cycle: ₹1,01,151 minus ₹1,00,000 = ₹1,151 per 10 grams",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-038",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1,
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-038s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Cycles in a year: about 6",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-039",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1,
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-039s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Total drag: about ₹6,906 per 10 grams, roughly 7% of spot, even though gold did not move",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-040",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-040s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "This is not a flaw in futures. The trader's margin money is not tied up in physical gold and can earn interest elsewhere, which roughly offsets the carry. But a trader who holds leveraged futures and leaves the rest of the cash idle feels only the drag. It is also why a gold ETF is often a simpler vehicle for long-term holding.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-042",
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "quiz",
              "title": "Who benefits from the curve?",
              "question": "Crude oil is in steady backwardation, and spot prices stay flat for six months. Which trader benefits most from the curve shape alone?",
              "options": [
                "A trader who stays long and rolls every month",
                "A trader who stays short and rolls every month",
                "Both benefit equally",
                "Neither, because spot did not move"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 0,
              "explanation": "In backwardation, each new contract is bought below spot and rises towards it as expiry approaches. A long who rolls repeatedly collects that convergence as positive roll yield. A short loses it."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-043",
              "_type": "keyFact",
              "label": "The most extreme contango on record",
              "value": "WTI May 2020 futures settled at minus $37.63 a barrel",
              "context": "On 20 April 2020, the COVID demand collapse filled US storage so completely that holders of the expiring contract paid to get rid of oil they could not store, while the June contract still traded around $20. Chapter 4 covers what this meant for MCX traders."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-044",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-044s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Basis for Hedgers: Why It Matters Beyond Speculation",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-045",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-045s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Hedgers rarely hold futures to expiry. They close when they buy or sell the physical commodity. So their real outcome depends on the basis on the day they close, not just the futures price they locked in.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-046",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "\\text{Effective price} = \\text{Futures entry price} + \\text{Basis when hedge is closed}",
              "caption": "Basis = spot minus futures on the day the hedge is lifted."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-053",
              "_type": "collapsible",
              "title": "Worked example: Suresh the jeweller revisited",
              "content": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-047",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-047s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Suresh buys December gold futures at ₹1,01,150 per 10 grams to hedge a purchase he will make in November. In November, spot is ₹1,04,000 and the December futures trade at ₹1,04,600. He buys physical gold and closes his futures.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-048",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1,
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-048s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Futures gain: ₹1,04,600 minus ₹1,01,150 = ₹3,450",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-049",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1,
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-049s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Physical cost: ₹1,04,000",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-050",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1,
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-050s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Net effective cost: ₹1,04,000 minus ₹3,450 = ₹1,00,550",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-051",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1,
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-051s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Check with the formula: basis at close = ₹1,04,000 minus ₹1,04,600 = minus ₹600, so ₹1,01,150 + (minus ₹600) = ₹1,00,550",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-052",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c2l3-052s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "His hedge locked in a price close to where he started, and the small remaining difference was the basis, not the level of gold.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-054",
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "numericInput",
              "title": "Read the basis",
              "prompt": "With 30 days to expiry, spot gold is ₹1,00,400 per 10 grams and the futures trade at ₹1,00,978. Using basis = spot minus futures, what is the basis in rupees?",
              "correctValue": -578,
              "tolerance": 0,
              "unit": "₹",
              "explanation": "₹1,00,400 minus ₹1,00,978 = minus ₹578. The negative basis shows futures above spot, which is contango. With 30 days left, it roughly matches 7% annual interest on ₹1,00,400 for 30 days."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-055",
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "scenario",
              "title": "Is the curve a forecast?",
              "scenario": "Aditya, a 26-year-old in Kochi who recently started trading, notices that the MCX crude oil contract for two months out is ₹150 per barrel below the near month. He messages his trading group: \"The market is telling us crude will fall ₹150. Easy money: short the far month and wait.\"",
              "prompt": "What is wrong with Aditya's reasoning, and what could actually happen to his short trade?",
              "modelAnswer": "What is wrong: a backwardated curve is not a forecast of falling prices. It usually reflects tight supply today and high convenience yield, which pushes near-month and spot prices above later months.\n\nWhat could happen: as time passes, the far contract Aditya shorted becomes the near contract, and it tends to converge towards spot. If spot stays flat, that contract rises towards spot, and his short loses money even though nothing dramatic happened. That is negative roll yield for a short in backwardation.\n\nThe better approach: form a view on crude from supply, demand and inventory, then use the curve to understand the carry cost or benefit of expressing that view. The curve shape tells you what it costs to hold a position, not where prices are going."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-056",
              "_type": "flashcardSet",
              "title": "Spot, Futures and Basis",
              "cards": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-056f1",
                  "term": "Spot price",
                  "definition": "The price for immediate delivery of a commodity today."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-056f2",
                  "term": "Basis",
                  "definition": "Spot price minus futures price. Negative in contango, positive in backwardation."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-056f3",
                  "term": "Cost of carry",
                  "definition": "Interest, storage and insurance for holding a commodity until a future date."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-056f4",
                  "term": "Contango",
                  "definition": "Futures above spot, with later months priced higher. Normal for gold and silver."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-056f5",
                  "term": "Backwardation",
                  "definition": "Futures below spot, with later months priced lower, usually because supply is tight now."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-056f6",
                  "term": "Convenience yield",
                  "definition": "The benefit of physically holding a commodity now, which can push futures below spot."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-056f7",
                  "term": "Convergence",
                  "definition": "Futures moving towards spot as expiry approaches, until basis is zero."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l3-056f8",
                  "term": "Roll yield",
                  "definition": "Gain or loss from convergence when holding and rolling futures; negative for longs in contango."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-057",
              "_type": "keyTakeaways",
              "points": [
                "A futures price is not a forecast. It is roughly spot plus cost of carry, minus convenience yield.",
                "Basis = spot minus futures. It is negative in contango and positive in backwardation.",
                "Contango is normal for storable, well-supplied commodities like gold. Backwardation signals tight physical supply.",
                "Futures converge to spot at expiry, so basis shrinks to zero.",
                "Roll yield means longs lose in contango and gain in backwardation, even when spot prices do not move.",
                "Hedgers' effective price = futures entry price + basis on the day they close the hedge."
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l3-058",
